{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. Johnson. I'm Dr. Patel, one of the hospitalists taking care of you. You came in tonight with chest pressure and shortness of breath, is that right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:35:12",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s correct. It’s been getting worse over the past few weeks, especially when I walk. I can only go about five minutes before the chest pressure starts."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:36:25",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And does it get better with rest or nitroglycerin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:37:03",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, the nitroglycerin helps. I’ve been using it more often lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:38:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. Your primary care doctor and cardiologist had been increasing your furosemide up to 120 mg daily, but you say it hasn’t helped much?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:39:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, not really. And now I feel lightheaded when I stand up. I think I might be too dry."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:40:35",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. We checked your labs and chest X-ray. No signs of fluid in the lungs, and your heart rhythm looks stable. Troponins are negative, so no active heart attack."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:41:50",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what’s causing the chest pain then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:43:05",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given your history of multiple stents and coronary disease, we’re concerned this could be worsening angina. We’re admitting you to monitor you and likely do a cardiac catheterization."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:44:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had a few of those before. Is it urgent?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2190-06-28 20:45:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s not an emergency tonight, but we want to do it early in the week. We need to see if there’s new blockage or progression of your disease."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2190-06-29 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I reviewed your labs. Your INR was high when you came in—above the therapeutic range. Since you're on warfarin for a prior pulmonary embolism, we held it temporarily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2190-06-29 08:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Should I be worried about clots?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2190-06-29 08:17:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. The pulmonary embolism was over six months ago, so the risk is lower. We’ll decide about restarting anticoagulation after the cath."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2190-06-29 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that’s a relief. My thyroid medicine—should I keep taking it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2190-06-29 08:20:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, absolutely. Your TSH was low, which suggests you might not be taking it consistently. It’s important to take levothyroxine every day, same time, on an empty stomach."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2190-06-29 08:21:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I admit I sometimes forget. I’ll do better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2190-06-30 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did another chest X-ray yesterday. No new changes—still no fluid in the lungs. Your breathing should not be from heart failure worsening."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2190-06-30 09:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s definitely my heart arteries again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2190-06-30 09:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s what we suspect. Your symptoms, history, and lack of improvement with diuresis point toward ischemia—your heart not getting enough blood during activity."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2190-06-30 09:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the cath will show that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2190-06-30 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. We’ll look at all your major arteries—LAD, circumflex, right coronary—and see if there are new blockages or restenosis in stented areas."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2190-07-01 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your INR has come down to a safer level. We’re planning your cardiac cath for tomorrow morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2190-07-01 10:01:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need another stent?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2190-07-01 10:02:25",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We won’t know until we see the images. If there’s a significant blockage causing reduced blood flow, they may stent it. But sometimes, we manage with meds if the blockage isn’t severe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2190-07-01 10:03:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve heard of something called FFR. Can they do that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2190-07-01 10:04:55",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, they can. It’s a pressure wire test to see if a blockage is actually limiting blood flow. It helps avoid unnecessary stents."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2190-07-02 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—your blood sugar is coming down with the insulin adjustments. We held metformin while you're in the hospital, which is standard."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2190-07-02 08:31:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My A1C was 8.0 recently. I know I need to do better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2190-07-02 08:32:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll work with you on that. The key is consistency with insulin, diet, and monitoring. We can arrange diabetes education after discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You just got back from the cath lab. I have the results."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:01:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "How did it go?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:02:25",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They found three-vessel coronary disease, which means blockages in all three main arteries. But the good news is, the blockage in your LAD was tested with a pressure wire—FFR was 0.85."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:03:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that good?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:04:50",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. An FFR above 0.80 means the blockage isn’t severely limiting blood flow, so they didn’t place a stent. Instead, we’ll optimize your medications."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:06:05",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So no more stents for now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:07:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not at this time. You’re doing well on medical therapy. We’ll focus on maximizing your antianginal and preventive meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:08:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my blood thinner? I was on warfarin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:09:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Since your pulmonary embolism was over six months ago and you’re not at high risk anymore, we’re stopping warfarin. You’ll stay on aspirin and clopidogrel for your stents."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That simplifies things. What changes to my other meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:12:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re increasing your isosorbide mononitrate from 30 mg to 60 mg daily. That should help with angina. Everything else stays the same, including your insulin and heart failure meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:13:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my GERD meds? I’m on both Nexium and Zantac."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2190-07-03 14:14:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re switching you to just esomeprazole once daily. Taking both isn’t usually necessary. Your outpatient team can reassess if symptoms return."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2190-07-04 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs this morning look great—kidney function improved, no anemia, and your blood counts are stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2190-07-04 09:01:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I’m going home today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2190-07-04 09:02:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. You’re stable, no chest pain overnight, and your breathing is better. We’re discharging you with updated medications and a follow-up with your cardiologist in one week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2190-07-04 09:03:35",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I should keep using nitroglycerin if the chest pain comes back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2190-07-04 09:04:50",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. One tablet under the tongue every five minutes, up to three. If it doesn’t go away, call your doctor or go to the ER."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2190-07-04 09:06:05",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And no warfarin, increase the Imdur, and just one acid reducer. Got it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2190-07-04 09:07:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. Keep taking your levothyroxine daily, monitor your blood sugar, and follow up with your primary care and cardiology teams."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2190-07-04 09:08:35",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel much more confident now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2190-07-04 09:09:50",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and don’t hesitate to call if anything changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2190-07-04 10:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a quick check before discharge. Any chest pain or shortness of breath since last night?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2190-07-04 10:16:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "None at all. I even walked down the hall earlier without any issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2190-07-04 10:17:25",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s excellent. Your vitals are stable—blood pressure 138 over 75, heart rate 76. Lungs have just trace crackles, much improved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2190-07-04 10:18:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m ready to go home then."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2190-07-04 10:19:55",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll have your discharge papers and prescriptions ready shortly. A nurse will go over everything with you before you leave."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2190-07-04 11:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One last thing—your furosemide is back to 40 mg daily. Since you were over-diuresed before, we’re not increasing it unless symptoms return."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2190-07-04 11:31:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. I’ll watch for swelling or trouble breathing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2190-07-04 11:32:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And keep your activity level moderate—no heavy lifting or straining for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2190-07-04 12:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The discharge order is in. You’ll be leaving within the hour."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2190-07-04 12:46:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Appreciate all the care, Doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2190-07-04 12:47:25",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Our pleasure. Stay well, and see you in follow-up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2190-07-04 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just confirming—you understand to stop warfarin and increase isosorbide mononitrate to 60 mg?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2190-07-04 13:01:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, stop Coumadin, take two Imdur tablets daily, and continue all other meds as listed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2190-07-04 13:02:25",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. And call your doctor if chest pain lasts more than 15 minutes or worsens."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2190-07-04 13:03:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will do. Thank you again."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2190-06-28 20:34:00",
    "admission_end": "2190-07-04 17:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with worsening exertional chest pressure and shortness of breath unresponsive to increased diuresis, raising concern for unstable angina. Initial evaluation showed no ischemic changes on EKG, negative troponins, and a normal chest X-ray. He had a history of three-vessel coronary disease with multiple prior stents and a pulmonary embolism six months prior. During the hospital stay, his INR was initially elevated, so warfarin was held. He underwent cardiac catheterization, which confirmed three-vessel coronary disease but showed a non-flow-limiting lesion in the LAD with FFR of 0.85, avoiding the need for stenting. Medical management was optimized, including increasing isosorbide mononitrate to 60 mg daily and discontinuing warfarin after completing a six-month course for pulmonary embolism. The patient remained stable, with resolution of symptoms, and was discharged home with follow-up arranged with cardiology.",
    "problems": [
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "Intermediate coronary syndrome",
      "Chronic diastolic heart failure",
      "Congestive heart failure, unspecified",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Personal history of pulmonary embolism",
      "Long-term (current) use of anticoagulants",
      "Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty status",
      "Long-term (current) use of insulin"
    ]
  }
}
